module

-- **PROVEN for the literal kinds it covers** -- which for a calculus over
-- Booleans alone is all of them. Adding a literal kind to the signature adds a
-- theorem here, and to that extent the file becomes open; what is here has no
-- `sorry`. A signature install leaves it alone.
--
-- **Canonicity** — that evaluating a literal gives a value in normal form.
--
-- The semantics keeps values canonical: bit-vectors reduced modulo their width,
-- maps ordered and without redundant entries. A rule reasoning about a literal
-- needs to know the model produces *that* form, not merely some representative
-- of the same value.
--
-- One theorem per literal kind the calculus has. The Boolean case is proven
-- below; add the others as the signature grows.
--
-- Could arrive proven: canonicity is a property of the SMT-LIB value types,
-- which come from `smt.eos` and are the same for every calculus over ordinary
-- SMT-LIB.
--
-- What this obligation cost Logos, and what shape the work takes, is in the
-- Logos experience report:
--   @EUDAIMONIA@/blob/main/docs/logos-experience-report.md
-- `SmtModel` directly rather than `ModelWf`: this statement does not need the
-- projections, and `ModelWf.lean` is one of the files `--theorems` can leave
-- out. A front-end theorem must not depend on another being generated.
public import @CALCULUS@.SmtModel
import all @CALCULUS@.SmtModel

@[expose] public section

namespace Smtm

/--
A Boolean literal evaluates to a canonical value.

Proven, and the proof is the whole pattern: evaluation of a literal is a
definitional unfold, and canonicity of the resulting value follows. Adding
`Numeral`, `Rational`, `Binary` or a structured kind means another theorem of
this shape -- though a structured kind is where it stops being one line, because
canonicity of a map or a sequence is a real property rather than a reduction.
-/
theorem model_eval_boolean_canonical (M : SmtModel) (b : SmtEval.native_Bool) :
    __smtx_value_canonical (__smtx_model_eval M (SmtTerm.Boolean b)) = true := by
  simp [__smtx_model_eval, __smtx_value_canonical]

end Smtm
